Please note that, as per Microsoft Pex's documentation, you have to run the framework in the debug mode to view the observations. You can also find more details about the 'PexObserve' in the Microsoft Pex's documentation:
http://research.microsoft.com/en-us/um/cambridge/projects/pex/default.aspx
If you want to use a different version of a library or framework, you need to replace the ArrayList in the namespaces used in the provided code above with a substitute from your own implementation or the desired version of the library or framework that you want to specify. The provided tests are contracts for ArrayList class from the .NET Framework.
Furthermore, you have to include the PexAPIWrapper.cs file in your project. This file is the adapter between Microsoft Research Pex static analysis driver and the ArrayList class in your project. Please refer to it and modify it according to your needs. As an example, the provided code assumes that the method 'Add' of ArrayList class returns an int and throws InvalidCastException and IndexOutOfRangeException.


A: The class you are looking for is Microsoft.Pex.Framework.Contracts.ContractFor. This is what it does says about it:

The ContractFor class is a base class that can be used to write contracts on your classes.

and further down it describes how to use it:

ContractFor provides static methods for writing invariants and predicates for your classes that you annotate using the [PexAssembly] attribute. 

You annotate the contracts with [PexAssembly] attribute, like:
[PexAssembly]  
public class MyClass  
{  
    ...  
    [PexMethod]  
    public void MyMethod(...)  
    {  
        ...  
    }  
    ...  
 }

and then you write your contracts using methods from ContractFor:
[PexAssembly]
public class MyClass
{
    ...
    [PexMethod]
    public void MyMethod(...)
    {
        Contract.Requires(ContractFor.Method(this.MyMethod, x1, x2, ...).Precondition);
        ...
        Contract.Ensures(ContractFor.Method(this.MyMethod, x1, x2, ...).Postcondition);
        ...
    }
    ...
}

The Contract.Requires and Contract.Ensures expressions are used to define what the method's precondition and postcondition should be. And Pex will verify these conditions.

A: The Contract Class in .NET is used to specify the conditions that a method should meet throughout its operation. These conditions are called Invariants and Postconditions. 
Here’s how to use the Contract Class:


*

*To specify precondition before method execution, use Requires method (specifies the condition that must be met).

*To verify method execution, use Ensures method which specifies an
additional check that is executed after the method body executes. 

*To describe a precondition or postcondition as a logical AND of multiple conditions, you may use a Requires/Ensures as a block of statements.


Here is an example:

public void MyMethod(int x, int y)
{
    Contract.Requires(x > 0);
    Contract.Requires(y > 0);
    Contract.Ensures(Contract.Result > x * y);

    Console.WriteLine(x * y);

}

In this example, Pex analysis will test the method with suitable positive numbers as input, also Pex will test whether result of multiplication is greater than input values.
Pex will not execute the method, it only verifies the contracts. To test contracts, select the code and press Ctrl + Alt + C, a dialog will appear asking whether you want to test. Click ok for the test to start.
Note that to use the Contract Class, you should reference the System.Diagnostics.Contracts assembly in your project.
If you want comprehensive code contracts, you can use the Contract Class for method contracts and the ContractFor class as a supplement for contracts at the parameter level.
These classes can be used to write preconditions, postconditions, and object invariants for methods and classes. They provide a high degree of robustness, enabling developers to specify behavioral contracts for their code.
Regarding ArrayList Contract Tests, the contract tests are not directly available with Pex API for this class. In practice, we have to write contract tests by manually creating a separate testing class which includes Pex annotations and contract conditions.
